organic food is no longer a niche market  [Applause]  in just 10 years sales of organic food  in the eu have more than doubled from  16.3 billion euros in 2008 to 37.4  billion euros in 2018. but while we're  buying more and more organic at the  grocery store do we even know what food  manufacturers actually mean by organic  [Music]  is an overall system of farming  management and food production that  combines best environmental practices  a higher level of biodiversity the  preservation of natural resources the  use of higher animal welfare standards  and the use of natural substances and  processes  great let's break it down then starting  with food crops  [Applause]  conventional farming uses lots of  nitrogen phosphorus and potassium-based  fertilizers on organic farms food is  grown with natural fertilizers such as  compost and seaweed products  farms need to keep weeds and bugs off  their crops normally they'll use  synthetic pesticides such as glyphosate  and prosulfocarb but in order to be  certified as organic in the eu farmers  have to stop using those pesticides use  natural techniques such as crop rotation  and predatory insects like ladybugs and  naturally derived pesticides made from  plants animals microorganisms or  minerals  it's important to remember though that  just because something is natural it  doesn't automatically make it harmless  to either people or the environment  to be certified organic your food cannot  contain products made from genetically  modified crops when it comes to organic  meat and other animal products the  animals are to be raised on 100 organic  feed  the animals we eat or whose products we  consume need to be kept disease free  if in a  conventional  sometimes antibiotics are given as a  present preventive tools in organics  antibiotics can be given as a last  resort if there are no other methods to  intervene normally the the  higher animal welfare standards applied  in organics  already keep animals in a healthier  status that prevent the use of  antibiotics  excessive antibiotics are not good for  people or animals because they can  create resistant germs or superbugs  the eu sees around 33 000 deaths related  to antimicrobial resistance every year  finally  organic livestock must have access to  the outdoors this isn't compulsory in  conventional farming  now all of these are just some of the  requirements the eu has a whopping  84-page report that explains organic  food rules  so that's what organic food is  now here's what organic food isn't  organic doesn't mean locally grown  europeans are the second largest  consumers of organic products in the  world local supply can't meet demand yet  so a large number of organic products  are imported  natural green or eco doesn't mean a  product is organic  and organic also doesn't necessarily  mean that a product is good for you  if you see this logo it means it's  certified as organic in the eu  that means eu rules have been followed  in its production processing handling  and distribution and that it contains at  least 95 organic ingredients  these rules are the same for all member  states  but these rules will change soon  in 2022 we'll have legislation with  stricter controls  non-eu and eu producers will have to  respect the same rules  and europe will have some new ambitious  targets as part of its farm to fork  strategy  by 2030 the eu wants to reduce the use  and risk of harmful chemicals and  hazardous pesticides by 50  and by the same year 25  of all eu farmland is to be dedicated to  organic farming  in 2019 it was only around 8  the eu's farm to fork strategy aims to  make healthy sustainable food more  accessible and affordable to all  europeans  because buying organic food is still too  expensive for many  organic farming is far from perfect but  many experts agree that if we develop  new techniques and manage food waste  better organic farming could provide the  entire world with better food in the  future  the future 